Decisions (worker's own, k259): (1) Agent marker = frontmatter key 'unreagent-hash: sha256:<64 hex>', the last key; rules marker = '<!-- unreagent-hash: sha256:<64 hex> -->' as the file's LAST line, so line 1 stays free for a rules frontmatter (paths:) should the text ever get one, and the heading stays first. Hash = SHA-256 of the file with the marker line taken out; CRLF is read as LF before checking, so a git autocrlf checkout on Windows stays ours. Exactly one marker line, else foreign. (2) Ownership as implemented: absent -> write; one marker + hash matches -> ours: identical (after CRLF->LF) left untouched, different replaced via writeFileAtomic (temp file in the same dir + rename); anything else -> never written, never deleted, all such paths on ONE WARN line per start. Sweep: regular files unreagent-*.md in <.claude>/agents not in the shipped set are removed only with a valid agent marker; no marker / bad hash / symlink -> left, not warned (not in our way). (3) Turning it off: setupSkills today returns before touching skills.dir when materialize is off, so materialized skills stay on disk. Same for agents: skills.agents false or materialize off removes nothing; files of an earlier start stay. skills.agents false logs one line (materialize off logs nothing, as today: it is the default). (4) Agents are written even when the skill materialization warned (a missing skill only loses its pre-load). (5) Wording check: rules.md and the agent bodies say the skills lie under .claude/skills/ — true, since agents are written only when skills.dir is <x>/.claude/skills and the agent/rules files go into that same <x>/.claude. Directory match is exact-case (.claude, skills). (6) New files get 0600 on Linux (writeFileAtomic's default for a new file); irrelevant on Windows.

